  "mini_lesson": [
    "In Spanish, every noun is either masculine or feminine. This decides which word you use for \"the\". Masculine nouns use \"el\" and feminine nouns use \"la\". So \"the\" becomes el or la, depending on the noun.",
    "Rule: most nouns ending in o are masculine and take el. Most nouns ending in a are feminine and take la. For more than one, use los with masculine nouns and las with feminine nouns, and add an s to the noun.",
    "Example: libro ends in o, so it is el libro, and many books are los libros. Casa ends in a, so it is la casa, and many houses are las casas.",
    "To check, look at the last letter of the noun. The letter o means el or los. The letter a means la or las. Then ask if there is one thing or many. Many things need an s on the noun and los or las."
  ],
